<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">B. J. Habibie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bacharuddin Jusuf Habibie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">B. J. Habibie was an Indonesian aeronautical engineer and politician who served as the country's third president from May 1998 to October 1999. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s minister of research and technology and his vice-president, and succeeded him when Suharto resigned amid the Asian financial crisis. In his brief presidency he freed the press, released political prisoners, prepared free elections and allowed the 1999 referendum in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> voted for independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/b-j-habibie.jpg" alt="B. J. Habibie" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Office of the Vice PresidentThe Republic of Indonesia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B._J._Habibie,_President_of_Indonesia_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bacharuddin Jusuf Habibie was born on 25 June 1936 in Parepare, South Sulawesi, the son of Alwi Abdul Jalil Habibie, an agriculturist of Bugis-Gorontalese descent, and R. A. Tuti Marini Puspowardojo, a Javanese noblewoman from Yogyakarta. He began to study aviation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> but, because of the dispute between the two countries over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-new-guinea">West New Guinea</a>, moved to the Technische Hochschule Aachen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where he took an engineer's degree in 1960 and a doctorate in aerospace engineering in 1965. In 1962 he married Hasri Ainun Besari, a medical doctor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined Messerschmitt-Bölkow-Blohm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, where he worked on the Airbus A-300B and reached a middle management post in 1974. That year President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> recruited him to return as part of a drive to industrialise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Two years later he became head of the new state aircraft maker, known as IPTN, which built Puma helicopters and CASA planes and in 1995 produced a small passenger aircraft, the N-250, a commercial failure.</p><p>From 1978 he was state minister of research and technology. By 1991 he oversaw ten state-owned industries, among them shipbuilding, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, arms and energy; an estimate of 1993 put their cost at nearly 2 billion dollars a year in state funds. In 1990 he was elected the first chair of the Indonesian Association of Muslim Intellectuals, a modernist organisation that gave him a political base. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> signalled in January 1998, during the Asian financial crisis, that he wanted him as vice-president, the rupiah fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As president he also forbade the official use of the terms pribumi and non-pribumi, lifted restrictions on the teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandarin-chinese">Mandarin Chinese</a> and passed laws giving autonomy to regencies and cities. He had opposed independence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> and offered special autonomy; the armed forces' leaders were not consulted on his decision to hold a referendum. After the vote of 30 August 1999 pro-Indonesian militias killed and displaced large numbers of people, and on 12 September he accepted a peacekeeping force mandated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><p>A leaked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> call with his attorney general raised doubts about the investigation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>. The Bank Bali scandal of 1999, in which bank funds reached members of his re-election team, damaged him further. In October 1999 the assembly known as the MPR rejected his accountability speech by 355 votes to 322, and he withdrew his candidacy; Abdurrahman Wahid succeeded him. He died in the Gatot Soebroto Army Hospital on 11 September 2019 and was buried at the Kalibata Heroes' Cemetery beside his wife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “B. J. Habibie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor, B.J. <b>Habibie</b>, allowed a referendum in East Timor in August 1999, in which voters chose independence amid violence by militias backed by the army; the territory became independent in 2002.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parepare, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Gatot Soebroto Army Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-4.02,119.62&amp;year=1989" aria-label="Parepare on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7869 2314 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of B. J. Habibie"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8319 2584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8319 2584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-4.02,119.62&amp;year=1989">What was here in 1989? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
